#!/usr/bin/env python3
# omarchy-session — save and restore your Hyprland windows across reboots.
#
# Part of the eric.session-restore Omarchy plugin. Inspired by and derived
# from wbarakat/omarchy-session-restore (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Waleed); see LICENSE.
#
# Usage:
#   omarchy-session save [--final] [--quiet]
#   omarchy-session restore [--dry-run] [--force] [--file F | --prev] [--workspace N ...]
#   omarchy-session status | enable | disable | daemon
#   omarchy-session install-hook | uninstall-hook
#
# Design notes
#   * Launch plans are computed at SAVE time (while /proc still describes
#     the running processes) and stored as argv lists. Restore never passes
#     a saved string through `sh -c`; argv lists are shlex-quoted before being
#     handed to Hyprland's exec dispatcher.
#   * A saved session is restored at most once: it is moved into restored/
#     before anything is launched, and its hash is recorded in
#     last-restore.json. Sessions saved by the *current* Hyprland instance are
#     never restored (so restarting omarchy-shell is harmless).
#   * Desktop notifications are fixed sentences (counts, or how old a stale
#     session is). notify-send arguments are visible to other local accounts
#     in the process list, so saved window titles and other session text never
#     go there. Unmatched titles stay in the mode-600 session log.

